WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.360
- Back at it with the
Doctor Reacts series.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.704
This week, I'm tackling "Scrubs."

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:07.537
(energetic music)

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.870
This show is supposed to be a comedy,

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.300
but I've also heard that it's rated

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.100
one of the most medically
accurate shows out there.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.764
Let's find out.

00:00:17.764 --> 00:00:19.900
(rock music)

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.200
Lie number 1: doctors
don't wake up at 6:00 a.m.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.830
We wake up at 4:00 a.m. or
5:00 a.m. at the lastest,

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.760
because usually, we have
patients scheduled for 6:00 a.m.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.650
Or if we're gonna be on a hospital shift,

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.880
you need to come in for
rounding and pre-rounding.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.200
I don't know what kind
of doctor this guy is,

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.738
but 6:00 a.m. is a bit late.

00:00:36.738 --> 00:00:41.738
(rock music plays)
(sirens wine)

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.360
- And four years of pre-med,
four years of med school,

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.480
and tons of unpaid loans have
made me realize one thing.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.390
- Good, could you go drop an
NG tube on the patient in 234,

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.940
and call the attending that
the lavage is positive?

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.580
- I don't know jack.
- (chuckles)

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:58.020
Your first day in the
hospital is a scary day.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:59.780
And I'm not talking about
when you're a medical student

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:00.910
on your clinical rotations

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.050
because no one really
expects much from you

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.410
when you're a medical student.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.130
When you go in as a
resident, you're a doctor,

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.500
you have a medical degree,
so people have expectations

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:11.710
that you'll know what you're doing.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:13.960
Now, just because you have the knowledge

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.030
from a textbook on how
to accomplish something,

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.480
it doesn't mean that you
have firsthand experience

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.620
and hands-on experience really matters,

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.700
especially when you're
dealing with other people.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.510
Because medicine is about
dealing with people,

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.380
not disease.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.550
- The hospital doesn't want

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:27.710
to be sued
- (laughs)

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:28.670
- Being sued is not

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:29.620
a good thing
- (chuckles)

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:30.840
- Finally, doctors,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.760
if there is a mistake

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.620
don't admit it to the patient.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:35.910
- Here's a fun fact about this:

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.250
common sense-wise, you may think that yes

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.990
if you hide the fact
that you made a mistake,

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.650
you're less likely to get sued,

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.709
but what research has
actually shown is that

00:01:43.709 --> 00:01:46.550
if you're honest and come
clean about your mistake,

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.500
which doctors totally make mistakes

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.400
we're not robots.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.920
If you come clean about your mistakes,

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.330
and show the proactive steps you've taken

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:55.240
to prevent that mistake
form occurring again,

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.670
patients are less likely to sue you.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.490
Patient's families are
less likely to sue you.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:01:59.460
So it's about owning up.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.100
It's about being a good person.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.260
And if you're a human first,

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:03.490
you're less likely to get sued.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.260
- I'm a tool, I am a tool,
I am a tool, tool, tool,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.050
an unbelievably annoying tool.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:09.040
- Yeah.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:09.873
- Yeah.

00:02:09.873 --> 00:02:10.830
Finally, these are your beepers,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.596
from now on, they
control your entire life.

00:02:12.596 --> 00:02:14.220
- (chuckles) You would actually think that

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.940
because it's 2018

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.160
or at least when I was doing residency,

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.220
it was 2017, you wouldn't
have beepers anymore.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.053
Guess what?

00:02:21.053 --> 00:02:23.010
We still have those same pagers!

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:23.880
It's crazy!

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.490
I actually had the communications people

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.190
from the hospital send
the message to my phone

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:29.300
instead of to the pager,

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.570
and I actually had them change that.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.980
To me, it just felt way to old-school

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:35.040
to be walking around with
one of those pagers on me.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.240
- You've done this to cadavers before.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.910
So, this guy's alive, just
poke it through his skin.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.539
Poke it through! Now!
- (laughs)

00:02:39.539 --> 00:02:40.500
- (whistles) Time's up.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.700
Carla, would you do it for him, please?

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:42.563
I'm also gonna need an ABG.

00:02:42.563 --> 00:02:43.891
- Why are you telling her?

00:02:43.891 --> 00:02:44.873
- Shut up and watch.

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:46.530
- (snorts) Okay, this is
actually really funny.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.620
Very true that when you
go in your first day,

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.330
some of the senior
doctors will want to see

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.220
your ability to do some of the procedures,

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.710
like put an IV in,

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.700
to do an ABG, which is
an arterial blood gas.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.680
It's basically when
you go into the artery,

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:00.940
usually the radial artery,

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:02.030
which is at the end of your wrist.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:03.860
It's one of the harder sticks

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.750
because you can see veins,

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.620
the artery you actually
have to feel the pulse,

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.110
and then insert the needle based on

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.130
where you're feeling the pulse.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.800
- Doctor Dorian, can you tell me

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.010
what ailment most often--

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.310
- I think I'm gonna love rounds.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:17.731
It's like being on a game-show.

00:03:17.731 --> 00:03:19.130
(bell dings)
What is uremia?

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:20.120
- That's my boy!

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.300
- This is a cool example
of what rounds are.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.480
You basically have a list of patients

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.130
that your team is responsible for

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.070
and you go around with your entire team

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:30.260
with the attending being in charge,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:31.740
sometimes it's the chief of medicine,

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.741
and you walk around to
each patient's bedside

00:03:33.741 --> 00:03:36.900
and you discuss the case,
not only with the patient,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.110
but with the team as well.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.630
Sometimes you get the
doctor asking you questions.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.090
That's a form of pimping.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.240
They're trying to get you
to think outside the box.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:45.980
The way that I like to lead rounds is:

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.280
sometimes, we know already
what the patient has,

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:50.100
because it's written on our reports,

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:50.937
but I would say,

00:03:50.937 --> 00:03:53.140
"Well, what if this test
came back negative?"

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:56.620
or "What if Mr. Johnson
presented without the headaches?

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.600
What would change in your diagnoses?"

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.640
And that way, it can
facilitate how a person thinks

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.320
because the quality of a good doctor

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:04.480
is being able to adapt.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.950
It's to be able to think on the spot.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:07.470
Back in the day, they were very mean

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:08.680
about this pimping process,

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:09.950
and if you didn't get it right,

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:10.950
they would talk down to you,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.010
they would call you stupid.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.760
I don't see that negativity there anymore.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:15.240
And I think the whole process has become

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.370
a lot more positive reinforcement-based.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.542
- I'm going for it

00:04:18.542 --> 00:04:19.472
(grunts) Infection.

00:04:19.472 --> 00:04:20.433
- Infection?

00:04:20.433 --> 00:04:22.033
- That's my girl!

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.333
Moving on.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.083
- I knew the answer!

00:04:26.083 --> 00:04:26.916
- I'm sure you did.

00:04:26.916 --> 00:04:27.749
- I was just frazzled--

00:04:27.749 --> 00:04:28.582
- How could you not be?
- You know, thick--

00:04:28.582 --> 00:04:29.700
- Oh, I know, yeah.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:30.590
- Good.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:32.297
But thanks, if there anything
I can ever do for you, just--

00:04:32.297 --> 00:04:34.160
- You could let me take you
to dinner tomorrow night.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.360
- We're back to the sex.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.981
Even a comedy medical show

00:04:36.981 --> 00:04:38.860
that is funny on its own

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.310
still needs to have a
little sex thrown in there.

00:04:42.387 --> 00:04:44.145
(crowd gasps)

00:04:44.145 --> 00:04:47.592
(laughter)
(clapping)

00:04:47.592 --> 00:04:49.395
- What the hell are you doing?

00:04:49.395 --> 00:04:50.228
(record scratches)

00:04:50.228 --> 00:04:52.220
- All those newspapers
are very legitimate,

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.810
like, having a ton of
newspapers just sprawled about

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:55.990
that are very old.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:58.220
Having that horrible TV that's,

00:04:58.220 --> 00:04:59.540
like, sitting way to high up

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:00.910
so you have to jack your neck up.

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:01.860
You daydream a lot,

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.570
especially when you've
been up for 24 hours.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.620
So that part of this is very realistic.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:07.400
- Did you actually just page me

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.120
to find out how much Tylenol

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.284
to give to Mrs. Lensner?

00:05:10.284 --> 00:05:11.117
- (laughs)

00:05:11.117 --> 00:05:12.530
- I was worried that it could
exacerbate the patient's--

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:15.870
- It's regular strength Tylenol.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:17.080
Here's what you do:

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.290
Get her to open her mouth,

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.390
take a handful, and throw it at her.

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:22.377
Whatever sticks, that's
the correct dosage.

00:05:22.377 --> 00:05:23.280
- (laughs)

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.980
I'm gonna be honest and say that

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:26.530
I've done stuff like this.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:29.610
My first rotation going into residency was

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:30.600
on the surgical unit.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.960
And I texted senior
residents or attendings

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:34.210
for everything.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:36.117
And then after a few weeks, I realized

00:05:36.117 --> 00:05:37.660
"Why am I doing this?"

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.750
If I know it's the answer
and I've double-checked

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:40.840
that it's the answer,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.720
why am I asking for permission

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.070
when I have a Doctorate degree.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:47.210
So it takes some time
to build up confidence,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.210
not every person works at the same pace.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:51.300
So I totally get where he's coming from

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:52.470
asking about the Tylenol.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.346
- For the love of god, what?

00:05:55.346 --> 00:05:57.240
- It's just, do you
really think we should be

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.170
talking about this in front of her?

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.501
- Her? She's dead.

00:05:59.501 --> 00:06:00.400
(woosh)

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.233
- Oh my god.

00:06:01.233 --> 00:06:02.170
All right, this show's crazy.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:04.130
- If they find out the nurses
are doing your procedures

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.440
for you, your ass will
be kicked outta here

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:06.930
so quick it'll make your head spin.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.551
- Nurses do the majority of the procedures

00:06:09.551 --> 00:06:10.800
as it is now.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.650
If the procedure's too difficult,

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:13.930
you're the one that's responsible

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:15.870
to come in and solve that problem.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:16.980
If then you can't do it,

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.310
then you can escalate to
either another resident

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:20.940
or maybe the attending physician.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.700
But generally, nurses are
better at doing the procedures

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.120
than us because they do
them so much more often.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.140
So their hands are much more well-equipped

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:30.467
to do the procedures.

00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:31.915
- I'm not really dead.

00:06:31.915 --> 00:06:34.544
- (chuckles) This is suck a wicked show.

00:06:34.544 --> 00:06:35.498
(whip cracks)

00:06:35.498 --> 00:06:36.360
- Ow
(laughter)

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.140
And like that, I was back in high school.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:39.470
You see, surgical interns, they're all

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:40.303
slice 'em and dice 'em.

00:06:40.303 --> 00:06:41.600
They're the jocks.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.150
Medical interns, we're trained
to think about the body,

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:45.710
diagnose, test.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:46.980
The medical interns, well--

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.109
- Hey you got a stain.

00:06:48.109 --> 00:06:49.163
Ah ha-ha!

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:51.260
- We're the chess club.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:52.093
- (groans)

00:06:52.093 --> 00:06:55.310
Not exactly accurate, but
has some truth behind it.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:58.850
Surgical interns and residents
are much more practical,

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:00.700
they see a problem, they want to fix it.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.920
They want to go in and
cut, reassemble, suture,

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.597
whereas, as a medical intern or resident,

00:07:06.597 --> 00:07:08.270
you have to really think about:

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.030
Is this patient gonna
follow the treatment?

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:11.560
Is this medicine gonna interact

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:12.940
with all these other medications?

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.470
Sometimes, surgery will consult
us, as the medical team,

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.150
to come in and manage something
that very simple for us,

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:21.240
but to them it's something
that they don't do often.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.500
Whereas on the other hand,
we'll call for their help

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.370
on simple surgical procedures,

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:27.910
even like sutures if they
get too complicated for us.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:29.210
It's really about teamwork

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:31.723
and making it a balanced approach.

00:07:31.723 --> 00:07:32.556
(pager beeps)

00:07:32.556 --> 00:07:33.389
- My first code.

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:34.460
See, here's how it works:

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:35.410
Someone's heart fails,

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:36.370
they beep everyone,

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:37.850
the first doctor in has to run the room,

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:38.770
tell everyone what to do,

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.560
basically decide if the
patient lives or dies.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:42.693
What am I, crazy?

00:07:44.141 --> 00:07:44.974
(door slams)

00:07:44.974 --> 00:07:45.807
(gasps)
- (gasps)

00:07:45.807 --> 00:07:47.030
- In general, you have a code team

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:49.580
that's set to respond to these code blues.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.130
Yes, it's about you gets there first,

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:51.963
but it's not about,

00:07:51.963 --> 00:07:53.080
"Any doctor in the hospital

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:55.100
should just run up and
start treating the patient."

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.130
We have teams dedicated for this,

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:59.560
'cause imagine if everyone's
busy and no one responds.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:01.250
What, the person just ends up dying?

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.220
Not the case.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.350
There's strict responsibilities here.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:06.008
In my hospital, the way it worked was

00:08:06.008 --> 00:08:09.220
the ICU on-call teams
usually ran the code,

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:11.020
and if there was something else going on,

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:13.350
then the medical team
came in and ran the code.

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:16.570
Not gonna lie, it was scary
when you would have one code

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:19.490
and then another code
happened simultaneously,

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:21.440
and then maybe a rapid response,

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:23.380
meaning that someone's heart didn't stop

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:24.670
but something serious is happening

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.690
where their heart may stop soon,

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.840
and at the same time you get an admission.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.483
It can get really hectic.

00:08:34.712 --> 00:08:36.430
- Hey, Champ.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:39.121
First night on call starts soon, huh?

00:08:39.121 --> 00:08:40.887
Gosh, you must be excited.

00:08:40.887 --> 00:08:42.167
- (screams)

00:08:42.167 --> 00:08:43.000
You betcha.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.770
- The first night I was on call,

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:46.830
I was terrified.

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:49.050
'Cause you have one senior attending,

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:50.520
and if they get busy with something,

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:52.830
it's all on you, and you're so worried

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:54.290
about making the wrong decision.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:56.990
But if you double-check
everything that you do,

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:58.990
speak to people who have
more experience than you

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:01.080
when you aren't sure about what to do,

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:01.913
you're gonna be fine.

00:09:01.913 --> 00:09:04.250
While those nerves are
totally normal and expected,

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:05.760
there are ways to combat it.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.840
By being informed, by being knowledgeable,

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:09.255
and by being smart. (laughs)

00:09:09.255 --> 00:09:11.681
(pager beeps)
- (sighs)

00:09:11.681 --> 00:09:14.880
(somber music)

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.210
- I can relate to the
feeling of coming on call

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:18.850
and instantly getting things to do.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:21.060
For me, my calls start at 7:00 p.m.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.470
So when I would go in at 7:00 p.m.,

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:26.190
the team that's coming off would hand off

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:27.023
the pager to me and say,

00:09:27.023 --> 00:09:29.620
"Hey, we were so behind that there's still

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.110
three or four admissions that
are in the emergency room

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.730
that need to be done and
brought into the hospital."

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:37.600
So you come in, and you
already have stuff to do.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.380
And then on top of that,
you're getting pages

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:40.970
about new things that are going on.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:43.410
- The attending thinks it
was a pulmonary embolism.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.340
No way anyone could have caught it.

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:46.980
Anyhow, you have to pronounce him.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:48.430
- Why didn't anybody page me?

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:51.260
- Could you just pronounce
him so I can go home?

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:52.580
- Okay, so this is an interesting thing.

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:54.510
Pronouncing someone dead is usually done

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:56.500
by the on-call resident,
so that's accurate.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.820
What happens is if a person
dies in the hospital,

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:00.430
the nurse pages the on-call resident,

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:02.030
they have to do a full exam.

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:04.040
We look at the pupillary response,

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:05.140
pain threshold,

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:08.390
we put a pen on the nail bed
and we press very heavily

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:11.290
so if the patient is somehow not dead

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:12.123
they would wake up.

00:10:12.123 --> 00:10:13.930
We listen to the heart,
we listen to the lungs

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:15.340
for an extended period of time.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:17.100
There's like this whole
physical exam that we do.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.450
And then, we write it in a long note

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:22.180
calling the time of death,
suspected cause of death.

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:24.060
In addition to that, once
you pronounce the patient

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.560
you have to talk to the family,

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.450
which is a very difficult conversation.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.670
You mature very quickly
during these moments.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:30.977
- Even now, when I finally get to go home,

00:10:30.977 --> 00:10:32.270
in the back of my head I'll know

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:34.250
the hospital's still here, wide awake.

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:35.760
- That line is very true.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.380
There's been times I was
in a good mood to go home

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:39.213
and I was like,

00:10:39.213 --> 00:10:40.300
"Let me just stroll through the ER."

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:42.700
And something happened and I
needed to help someone out,

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:46.020
and I end up staying three,
four, five hours past my shift.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:47.160
It's one of those things.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.610
Go home while you still can,

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:50.850
'cause you need to be
back here the next night

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:51.700
to save lives.

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:53.160
- The most important thing
is that I got through

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:54.490
my first three days without looking

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:56.760
like a complete idiot.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:57.863
(thuds)

00:10:57.863 --> 00:10:58.820
- (snorts)

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:01.104
Another episode of the
Doctor Reacts series

00:11:01.104 --> 00:11:02.420
is in the books.

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:05.200
Mixed feelings on this
one, and that's surprising

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:07.010
'cause I thought I would
find it really funny.

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:08.860
There was definitely
parts that had me laughing

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:09.693
and I was like,

00:11:09.693 --> 00:11:10.710
"Yeah, that's so true."

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:11.930
But I don't know, some parts were just

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:13.810
so dark and mean-spirited

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:15.000
that I couldn't get behind it.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.030
It pushes a lot of the stereotypes

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:20.130
on the medical community that
aren't good to be pushing.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:21.130
We should be getting rid of them

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:22.220
instead of celebrating them.

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:23.880
I probably need to watch more episodes

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.700
to truly understand what the
characters are all about.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.410
If you like this type of content,

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:29.910
please subscribe down below.

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:32.500
'Cause it tells YouTube that
you really enjoy this content,

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:35.130
and it allows me to continue
making this type of content.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:36.650
And if you have a show that you love,

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:39.210
or specific episodes
of the "Scrubs" series

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:40.350
that you want me to watch,

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:42.060
leave it down below in the comments.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:44.660
As you know, I react to
the comments all the time.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.090
If not in the comments section,

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:48.150
in my monthly responding
to comments video.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:50.731
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:11:50.731 --> 00:11:53.481
